{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Imperative Languages in Software Programming", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 3, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 1, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A1.2", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words you use to talk about Imperative Languages in Software Programming", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Understand key information about the concepts, principles, and methodologies in simple spoken and written situations.  Talk about programming techniques, use cases, kinds, and sequences in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures.  Identifies general aspects related to Software Programming in short written or spoken conversations about past experiences.", "type": "secondary_1"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob03re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "command", "type": "name"}, {"content": "an order or instruction", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: An imperative programming language is, basically, a list of commands for the computer to perform. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob03aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "paradigm", "type": "name"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "a model, or parameter to do something", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: For today's program, we decided to bring into discussion the imperative languages paradigm in computer science. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob03aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "logical", "type": "name"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "a credible way of thinking \treasonably", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: That's why programmers fix logic statements orderly and descriptively for the computer to execute. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob03aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "enable", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to make something possible by itself", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The imperative paradigm is very important because it enables all elemental computer functionalities to work. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob03aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "goal", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the expected results of an action", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The imperative paradigm focuses on the processes the program performs rather than on the goals that the program needs to achieve.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob03aud06", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Look for information about two imperative programming languages.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write a brief description of each one of the languages. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Use indefinite pronouns, infinitives and present simple. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Share your work with your partner.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"content": "I look for information about programming languages. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I write a brief description of those languages. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I Use indefinite pronouns, infinitives and present simple. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I share my work with my partner.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Indefinite Pronouns", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><b>Indefinite Pronouns</b> are words that replace unspecific general nouns; use them when you want to refer to an overall noun.</p>", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">There are different <b>indefinite pronouns</b> for either singular or plural nouns. Today we are going to focus on two common indefinite pronouns: <b>All</b> and <b>both</b>.</p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\">&nbsp;<b>All</b></p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\">You use <b>all</b> when you are pointing to the whole amount of the noun you are talking about.</p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Example:</p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><i>We can&rsquo;t change one single program without modifying the others, as <b>all</b> need a special code. </i>(All the programs.)</p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><b><i>All</i></b><i> you need to understand how Java works is in the manual I gave you. </i>(All means all the tools)</p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><b>Both</b></p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Use both when you are relating two similar ideas; both is a plural pronoun, as it refers to two things.</p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Example:</p> <ul> <li style=\"text-align: justify;\"><b><i>A</i></b><i>: I don&rsquo;t know what to use to program this app, Java or C#.<br /> <b>B</b>: I think <b>both</b> are good options. </i>(both means Java and C#)</li> <li style=\"text-align: justify;\"><i>A program is made of <b>both</b> the design and the codification. </i></li> </ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "No, both stand for the same imperative paradigm; the difference is that the procedural programming uses the concept of blocks", "type": "example"}, {"content": "That's correct, all use them, specially high-level imperative languages such as Java or C#.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Infinitives after Certain Verbs", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">There are verbs that introduce other actions; Those verbs can introduce the actions with the infinitive, with the gerund, or, in some cases, with both of them. Let's have a look at the&nbsp;<b>verbs</b>&nbsp;that go with&nbsp;<b>infinitives</b></p>", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">There are many verbs that use the&nbsp;<b>infinitive</b>&nbsp;to introduce another action; some of those verbs are: want, need, agree, decide, and except, among others. Remember that you always need to use&nbsp;<b>the infinitive</b>&nbsp;after those verbs.&nbsp;</p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><br /> Note that the word <b><i>infinitive</i></b> refers to&nbsp;<b>the base form</b>&nbsp;of the verb accompanied by the preposition&nbsp;<b>to</b>&nbsp;as in <b>to buy</b> and <b>to program</b>.&nbsp;<br /> <br /> The structure is very simple:</p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><b>Subject + Verb + Infinitive + Complement</b></p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><i>For example:</i></p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><i>I <b>want to know </b>the difference between imperative and declarative languages.</i></p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><i>She <b>needs to implement </b>an imperative language to make the program works.</i></p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><i>We <b>expect to finish </b>programming this app soon.</i></p>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Kim, a software programmer, agreed to join us and help us to understand this topic. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "We decided to bring the imperative languages paradigm in computer science into discussion.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "template_override": 1, "stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "I can talk about the imperative languages in software pr