emplate_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "audio", "title": "", "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n2le01ob03re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"time": "0", "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><b>Mark</b>: For today's program, we decided to bring the imperative languages paradigm in computer science into discussion. So, Kim, a software programmer, agreed to join us and help us to understand this topic. Hi, Kim, tell us. What are imperative languages in software programming?<br /><b>Kim</b>: Thank you, Mark. Well, imperative languages are programming languages that work under the imperative paradigm, that is, the way we design programs. The imperative paradigm is very important because it enables all elemental computer functionalities to work.<br /><b>Mark</b>: Interesting! And, what is the imperative paradigm about?</p>", "image": "enpe7n2le01ob03re2img01.jpg", "id": 0}, {"time": "41000", "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><b>Kim</b>: The imperative paradigm focuses on the processes the program performs rather than on the goals that the program needs to achieve. Imperative languages describe how the program has to work; for that reason, an imperative programming language is, basically, a list of commands for the computer to perform. Consequently, Imperative programming consists of statements that modify the state of a program. That&rsquo;s why programmers fix logic statements orderly and descriptively for the computer to execute.<br /><b>Mark</b>: So, I suppose it's really taxing to program all those statements.<br /><b>Kim</b>: It used to be tiring, but with the advent of procedural programming, all became faster.<br /><b>Mark</b>: Is procedural programming different from the imperative programming paradigm?</p>", "image": "enpe7n2le01ob03re2img02.jpg", "id": 1}, {"time": "88500", "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><b>Kim</b>: No, both stand for the same imperative paradigm; the difference is that the procedural programming uses the concept of blocks to set the program, I mean, blocks are groups of statements that contain a whole procedure, while in the procedural programing&nbsp;the statements are separated.<br /><b>Mark</b>: I see. To conclude, Kim. I wish you could give us some examples of imperative languages.<br /><b>Kim</b>: Sure, Java and .Net Framework are clear examples of imperative languages, as both use variables that can be either internal or external.<br /><b>Mark</b>: Does that mean that all imperative languages use variables?<br /><b>Kim</b>: That&rsquo;s correct, all use them, especially high-level imperative languages such as Java or C#.</p>", "image": "enpe7n2le01ob03re2img03.jpg", "id": 2}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n2le01ob03re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar"}]}